==English==

===Pronunciation===
*:Rhymes: [[Rhymes:English:-ɛnɪk|-ɛnɪk]]

===Adjective===
'''telegenic'''
# having an [[appearance]] and exhibiting [[qualities]] thought to be [[attractive]] to [[television]] [[viewer]]s
# [[televisual]]

Commentary:  A excellent example of the sort of errant neologism that can be created when roots of existing words are combined into new structures without regard to their original meaning.  'Telegenic' first gained use in the 1980's among television commentators as a way of describing how charismatically various political candidates came across on television.  Ostensibly using 'photogenic' as a model and wishing to cite television rather than photography, the creator simply mashed the two together.   Sadly [[tele-]] meaning 'distance', and [[-genic]], meaning 'producing or generating' or 'suitable to be reproduced by' do not combine to mean 'looks good on TV'.  If anything, the roots of 'telegenic' mean 'looks good from a distance'.  This would have been a ''profoundly'' wry and witty commentary upon the nature of political candidates, if only we could afford the originator that much credit.  Instead, we can only curse him or her for interjecting more useless trash into our already befouled language.

A further irony is that there already existed a word that expressed 'looks good on camera' and so no need to create a new aberration of language - photogenic.   Photo- refers to light and not the mechanical means of recording it [which also takes its name (photography) by reference to light].  
